# route_feasibility.mtr
#
# The canonical example. A route is a trajectory through a network, and
# every network boundary is an interface that must be matched, not just
# crossed: an entity entering a new lane must meet the receiving lane's
# conditions (velocity within tolerance, inside a time budget). The engine
# evaluates criteria under variations and checks every interface.

EVALUATION route_feasibility
SUBJECT route
INPUTS depot_capacity driver_hours stops volume

# --- What passing means, field by field -------------------------------

CRITERION depot_within_capacity
    FIELD depot_capacity <= 0.95
    SEVERITY error
    NOTE depot utilization (0..1) must stay under 95%

CRITERION driver_within_hours
    FIELD driver_hours <= 14.0
    SEVERITY error
    NOTE driver operating hours must respect the 14-hour limit

CRITERION load_balanced
    FIELD volume <= 1.0
    SEVERITY warn
    NOTE normalized load factor; warn only

# --- Variations: the same route under edge conditions -----------------

VARIATION peak_volume
    ADJUST depot_capacity SCALE 1.2
    ADJUST volume SCALE 1.3
    NOTE winter peak: depot absorbs 20% more, volume up 30%

VARIATION driver_exhaustion
    ADJUST driver_hours OFFSET 1.0
    NOTE +1h if the driver reports fatigue at the first stop

# --- Interfaces: the ghost car ----------------------------------------

INTERFACE merge_onto_expressway
    FROM collector TO expressway
    MATCH velocity TO lane_velocity
    TOLERANCE 0.10
    BUDGET 20m
    TIME merge_t
    NOTE must match expressway lane velocity within 10% inside 20 minutes of hitting the ramp

INTERFACE exit_offramp
    FROM expressway TO surface
    MATCH velocity TO offramp_velocity
    TOLERANCE 0.25
    BUDGET 5m
    TIME offramp_t
    NOTE offramps are more forgiving: 25% tolerance, 5-minute budget

END